data.content:
## What breaks in .NET 11

In .NET 10, the Cosmos DB provider already **removed native synchronous LINQ support** but offered a temporary opt-in (`EnableSyncOverAsync()`) that internally wrapped async calls. **In .NET 11, that opt-in is removed entirely.**

Every synchronous call in your repository — `ToList()`, `First()`, `Count()`, `SaveChanges()` — will throw an **`InvalidOperationException`** at runtime.

## The fix: go fully async

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

**Key changes:**
- Return types → `Task<T>` / `Task`
- `ToList()` → `ToListAsync()` with `await`
- `First()` → `FirstAsync()` with `await`
- `Count()` → `CountAsync()` with `await`
- `SaveChanges()` → `SaveChangesAsync()` with `await`

The async LINQ methods are in `Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore` namespace. This change will cascade to callers — controllers/services must also become `async`/`await` up the call stack.
